A||N||22||Monkeyman||She's on the road!!!!!!--but I have some problems||||1||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||05-11-2000||02:59 PM||||I just got her back from the shop and <B>DAMN</B> does a v6 sound good!!!!!!!!!!  WOW!!!!!!  Whoopee!!!!!!!!!!!!!<P>Now for the bad news...<BR>I had to get new calipers on the rear because the bleeder screws broke off.  Oh, well.  Of course, I was planning on doing the GA upgrade (priced everything--$200 total not counting the machine work) so now I'll have some expensive, brand new stock calipers that will sit in my garage collecting dust.<P>While I was sitting in line at the bank, my temp gauge shot up to the red zone, then started to come back down slowly.  By the time I had gotten through the line and on my way again, it was back down to about 185.  Any thoughts??<P>Next, my dash lights wouldn't come on at 1st.  Finally, they came on but are very dim.  And of course the blue dingy thingy started chiming.  (I pulled it out as soon as I got home.)  Any thoughts on this one??<P>Last (for now), my left turn signal won't shut off automatically.  (Both are slow if it makes any difference.)  I can see the tach needle move slightly when the ts flashes.  Is this normal?  What can I do to get the left ts to cancel automatically?<P>Last (this time for real), the tranny (auto) doesn't want to shift into 2nd until about 3200rpms.  Is this normal?  Sorry, but I've never had a V6 before so I didn't really keep up on the V6 threads.  I know that I could do a search, but I don't have the time.  (I have to go back to work in a little while.  This will make my 2nd double this week not counting the triple I had to work last weekend.)<P>Thanks in advance, guys (and gal).  I appreciate all the help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Homicidal||05-11-2000||04:13 PM||||What year and model is your Fiero?<P>Sounds electrical totally.  Check the temp sensor, check the battery cables, the battery, the alternator, and the cables that run from the battery to other sections of the car.  Sounds like a loose connection to me, somewhere.<P>But I am only beginning to troubleshoot.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||05-11-2000||05:01 PM||||Congrats, MM!  You and me running in the same week... imagine that!  I guess it goes to show that even hard luck fiero stories, like ours, will sort themselves out... in time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>i'm in tears for you, man... sniff sniff<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||05-11-2000||05:13 PM||||As for trouble shooting, I'll give it a try.<P><B>Cooling:</B>  Check the coolant level.  It is possible that they installed the new engine and didnt properly fill the engine while haveing it run.  It hides a awfull lot of coolant in there.  Of course my car improved a whole lot after I replaced the water pump... would have to imagine they woulda checked that out b4 installing the engine.<P><B>electrical:</B> Definately sounds like a lose conection somewhere.  Check the battery, altenator, starter, and grounding blocks ect.<P>As for the turn signals, and the auto I can't help you.  Sorry.<P>Enjoy, Im going home to work on mine now.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||05-11-2000||05:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Monkeyman:<BR><B>I just got her back from the shop and [b]DAMN</B> does a v6 sound good!!!!!!!!!!  WOW!!!!!!  Whoopee!!!!!!!!!!!!!<P>  Congrats! Been a long time coming...<P>Now for the bad news...<BR>I had to get new calipers on the rear because the bleeder screws broke off.  Oh, well.  Of course, I was planning on doing the GA upgrade (priced everything--$200 total not counting the machine work) so now I'll have some expensive, brand new stock calipers that will sit in my garage collecting dust.<P>  The ones on the Monkeymobile would have been a direct replacement.<P>While I was sitting in line at the bank, my temp gauge shot up to the red zone, then started to come back down slowly.  By the time I had gotten through the line and on my way again, it was back down to about 185.  Any thoughts?? <P>  When you sit in traffic, the temp will climb to about 220* or so. Then the fan will kick on. Can't explain the red zone. Maybe some air still in the system?<P>Next, my dash lights wouldn't come on at 1st.  Finally, they came on but are very dim.  And of course the blue dingy thingy started chiming.  (I pulled it out as soon as I got home.)  Any thoughts on this one?? <P>  The control could be dirty, or there could be a loose connection at the transistor that controls the lites. What about your radio display? Same story? Do a search on dash lites. This has been touched on, several times.<P>Last (for now), my left turn signal won't shut off automatically.  (Both are slow if it makes any difference.)  I can see the tach needle move slightly when the ts flashes.  Is this normal?  What can I do to get the left ts to cancel automatically? <P>  The TS cancel is a mechanical function. There is a cam (or some such) in the steering column, that trips the lever off when you straighten the wheel. This is a common thing with GM cars. Never tried to fix one, though.<P>Last (this time for real), the tranny (auto) doesn't want to shift into 2nd until about 3200rpms.  Is this normal?  Sorry, but I've never had a V6 before so I didn't really keep up on the V6 threads.  I know that I could do a search, but I don't have the time.  (I have to go back to work in a little while.  This will make my 2nd double this week not counting the triple I had to work last weekend.)<P>  My V-6 shifts a bit higher than I would expect. Don't know about 3200, though. I'll have to check. Might be the TV (kickdown) cable. <P>  You might want to check the battery voltage. Make sure the alternator is charging it. Also check to make sure all the grounds were reinstalled. My gauges were really flaky until I tightened the ground on the forward cylinder head, just above the trans. <P>Thanks in advance, guys (and gal).  I appreciate all the help.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-11-2000||05:17 PM||||MM, Congrats! As for your temp problem, I would check the coolant level. You probably have air in the system and when it started to boil over is when the thermostat opened.<P>Dim Dash lights and dinging, Check your fuses. Sounds like a blown fuse or a shorted wire.<P>Turn signal problem requires the switch in the column to be replaced. Yes the volt meter will "tick" with the turn signal this is normal.<P>Lastly, Change the trans fluid and filter. This should correct your tranny problem. Actually it sounds like you are low on tranny fluid but idf the car sat a while I would change out the filter. <P>Great to see your driving it! So do you still want purple emblems for the doors?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT Bastard||05-11-2000||07:30 PM||||I've had a similar problem with my auto, and I would agree with Oreif.  Check the fluid! And remember to check it with the car running.  Does it look clean(pink in color?)||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||05-11-2000||07:54 PM||||HMMMMMMM, in case you can't tell, I'm grinning ear to ear to see that you are satisfied, Monkey.  Enjoy the ride, I know I did.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||05-11-2000||10:36 PM||||Temp. going up and down is the classic symptom of the cooling system needing burped. If it seems like the tranny is shifting pretty hard too, it's probably the TV cable needing adjusting. Does the 2-3 shift seem ok? I can tell Monkey is smiling! But remember, there are always lots of bugs to iron out of a neglected car that sat for a fair period of time. Good luck, we'll help you as much as possible.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Monkeyman||05-12-2000||09:29 AM||||The Grape Ape is an '85 GT for those that don't know.  The mechanic checked (and replaced) the battery cables and the alternator.  The motor isn't new.  It has 130k on it (I think--Bmaker??).  Seems to run well other than a slight exhaust leak where the Y pipe connects to the headers???  The coolant was checked just before I left the shop, but I'll be sure to check it again.  Can't check the lights on the stereo as I don't have one.  The lights behind the HVAC controls seem to be bright enough.  How tough is it to fix the ts problem?  Expensive?  Complex? What's the TV cable, where is it, and how do I adjust it?  Tranny fluid was checked just before I left the shop.  I know there's enough, but I would imagine that the filter hasn't been changed in 15 years!  Oreif--Email me about the emblems.  I kinda forgot what we're talking about.  BB-As far as I can tell the 2-3 shift is fine.  At least it shifts sooner than the 1-2 shift.<P>Yes, guys!  I think I'll need surgery to remove the smile from my face!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I have a ton of things that need work, but I want to get the important ones out of the way 1st.  After I'm sure that it will be fairly reliable, then I'll start working on the other things.  <P>I need to add a new problem.  The power windows are SSSLLLLLLOOOOOOOOOWWWWWWWW.  The passenger side works faster/better than the drivers side.  The drivers side won't even go up all the way (lacking about a 1/4") unless you open the door.  I assume this just relieves the pressure on the motor.  Is there something I can grease, or do I need a new motor?  I think I still have a spare window motor in the garage somewhere collecting dust.  I'm not sure if it's for the correct side, though.  I don't suppose they are interchangable.<P>Thanx again for all of the help.   You can't imagine how nice it is to get this.  (That made sense to me...) ||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||05-12-2000||09:50 AM||||The windows are direct feeds from the switchs. There are no relays in this circuit. Slow motors could be either a door problem or the switches. To find out wich it is without tearing things apart do this:<P><BR>disconnect the window harness where it comes out of the door's rubber tube. It's the large connector with 2 wires. It's right next to the end of the tube where it opens under the dash. You may have to pull the carpet down some to get to it.<P>Then stick a lenght of wire in each hole on the door half of the connector.<P>clamp one of these to the + terminal of a chaged battery. Tap the other against the negative terminal. If the window doesn't move reverse the leads.<P>If the window moves at proper speed like this then the switches are full of crud and not passing full power to the windows.<P>If the window is still slow it could be a number of problems inside the door. Could be anything from a simple adjustment to repair/replace a motor.<P>The switches can be cleaned but it's better for most people to just replace them. It's easy to get the switch apart, but not so easy to get back together. If it's not put together right it will not work, short out, or work backward.||||reg||1
Z||000011||GT Bastard||05-12-2000||10:14 AM||||About that tranny fluid.  Having too much can also cause your shifts to be irregular.  It'll cause the fluid to foam-up and lose it's lubrication properties. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||05-12-2000||10:41 AM||||I have another set of window switches (mounting tabs are broken off).  Maybe I'll try these 1st and see if it makes a difference.  Will the window motors exchange (side to side)?  I have a pair that came out of a set of donor doors.  One of the motors looks to be brand new, but the other is definitely old.  Unfortunately, I think the new one is for the passenger side.  I figured the motor might be bad since the drivers side is so much worse than the passenger side (I would assume that the drivers window is used more).  All I can remember about removing the motors is that it's a pain in the rear and I don't want to do it if I don't have to.  What is there to adjust?  I thought everything was part of a sealed unit.||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||05-12-2000||07:55 PM||||Things usually wear out faster on the driver power window. On my 85 GT, the passenger window was super slow and wouldn't roll all the way down. I took the inner panel off, lubed it all up, and now it works just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000014||BAM-BAM||05-12-2000||08:37 PM||||   Monkey, Great to see you are on the road in the Ape!!! As for your problems, you have alot of the same ones I had with my 85GT. My tranny still shifts at about 3200 RPM and has done so for the past three years that I have owned it. Not sure if this is bad, but if you find out that is, please let me know.<BR>You will undoubtedly have to replace the turn signal switch, the part inside the colum. If I remember right, it's not all that difficult. The multifunction switch (lever) wouldn't cause that problem. Overheating? Everyone is correct, check the fluid level. Also, a commonly ignored item to check is the thermostat and radiator caps. If they're cracked around the seals you could be loosing pressure. Dash lights; check your dash dimmer reostat. Mine was dirty from fifteen years of dust. cleaned it up, burnished the connection stabs, VIOLA, dash lights that worked. Same is probably true with your window switches. I just replaced mine and they worked fine thereafter. The drivers window was worse, probably because of more frequent use. Well, that's about all I have, let us know how things turn out.<P> Be Cool <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><BR> BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||12
Z||000015||Monkeyman||05-12-2000||10:04 PM||||Thanx.  I'll try my "extra" set of window switches in the morning.  If that doesn't make a difference, what should I lube?  I don't want to get grease on the windows.  <P>I can try the dimmer knob (?) from the Monkeymobile and see if that makes a difference.  If not, I guess I'll have to start hunting for a short.  Where would I start my search?<P>Seems like I remember an old post that said something about having to lift the rear (only) of the car to burp the cooling system correctly.  Is there any other way to do this?  I don't have the equipment to lift the rear any more than jack level.  I'll check the coolant level before I head to work.  Hopefully by next summer, I'll have the cash to install a new motor (probably a 3.1 or 3.2 as they seem to be a mite cheaper than something bigger).  When I do that, I'll probably replace everything else involved (radiator, coolant lines, etc.).  I just don't have the $$ to lunch the motor I have now.<P>Last question for the night...What do I lube to make my power locks work better?  I should be getting my new lock cylinders tomorrow, so I can lock the doors <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>Thanx again for all of the help, guys.  I really appreciate it.  With you guys helping  I don't have to be a millionaire to own a Fiero!  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||BAM-BAM||05-12-2000||11:07 PM||||  Monk,<BR>  Try this site [URL=http://www.xnet.com/~paulv]He also just came out with a new product for notchback Fiero's.... Just go to the site and click on the link to his Fiero lens's. The answer to your questions might be found in his tech tips section.<P> Hope this helps, BAM-BAM    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 05-12-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 05-12-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000017||Monkeyman||05-13-2000||10:05 AM||||I'll scope that out in a minute.  Got some lithium grease in a can this am.  Sprayed some on the parts of the window I could see (not much).  Pass side only as I have the inner door panel removed.  Seems to make a difference.  If I get a chance, I'll pull the drivers side off either today or tomorrow and see if it helps over there.  I used some on the locks, too.  Now it only takes a couple of tries to get the pass side lock to work fully (as opposed to 4 or 5).  I might try spraying some more grease on there this afternoon.||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||05-13-2000||11:02 AM||||Keep trying the lube Monkey, sounds like it might help out. I'd even spray it down a couple times with WD-40 first to clear out the old gunky lube first. Let it dry and lube with the lith grease a couple more times. It's very hard to reach all the places needing to be lubed. Work that grease in good, but don't run the window motors to much or they'll overheat. Let them cool down for a while and run them up and down some more. Don't run your batteru down too low...||||reg||1
Z||000019||Monkeyman||05-16-2000||10:29 AM||||All of the fuses are good.  I still don't have any console lights and I can see where the "brake" light (idiot light) dims slightly when the turn signal comes on.  I assume I have a short somewhere.  Where do I start to look?  What am I looking for specifically?  Any certain systems?||||reg||1
Z||000020||DJRice||05-16-2000||10:51 PM||||Monkey, <P>If fuses are ok, and dimmer switch is Ok, then it sounds like its time to pull the gauge pod rear cover off and check the light bulbs.  Also, look at the wire trace sheet on the back of the gauge set.  After sitting for a while, the laminate tends to pull off the wire contacts and you can loose connections which will cause light problems as well as some gauge problems.  <P>But check the light bulbs first.<P>As for the cooling, you dont need to jack the back of the car up to burp it right.  Just pull the thermostat housing off the back, then pull the radiator cap off the front.  Then start adding fluid in the back until you start to get a drip out of the radiator up front, then cap the radiator and continue to fill until you have fluid in the Thermostat housing. Finally, put the thermostat back in and cap back on then fill the resivoir and that should do it.<P>As for the turn signal, the part you need to replace is most likely available in the HELP! section at local auto parts store.<P>I just reread your posts, and I think you should look at the wire trace on the back of your gauge kit, Id be willing to bet your going to find some corrosion.<P>Hope this helps<P>||||reg||1
